## Proof

Consider the $2^{500}$ points on the circle, labeled $1, 2, \ldots, 2^{500}$ in some arbitrary order.

**Construction.** For each $i \in \{1, 2, \ldots, 2^{499}\}$, form the chord joining the point labeled $i$ to the point labeled $2^{500} + 1 - i$.

This gives $2^{499}$ chords. I verify the two required properties:

**Equal sums.** Each chord connects label $i$ to label $2^{500}+1-i$, so the sum of the two endpoint labels is
$$i + (2^{500}+1-i) = 2^{500}+1$$
for every chord. Thus all $2^{499}$ chords share the common sum $2^{500}+1$.

**Pairwise disjoint.** The first endpoints of the chords carry labels $\{1, 2, \ldots, 2^{499}\}$. The second endpoints carry labels $\{2^{500}+1-i : i = 1, \ldots, 2^{499}\} = \{2^{499}+1, 2^{499}+2, \ldots, 2^{500}\}$. These two sets are disjoint and their union is $\{1, 2, \ldots, 2^{500}\}$, so every label is used as an endpoint of **exactly one** chord. Therefore no two chords share an endpoint; the chords are pairwise disjoint.

**Selecting 100 chords.** Since $2^{499} \geq 2^7 = 128 > 100$, among the $2^{499}$ pairwise disjoint chords constructed above, we may select any 100. These 100 chords are pairwise disjoint and all have the same sum $2^{500}+1$.

$\blacksquare$

